[[ ## reasoning ## ]]
The Template Method pattern defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a method, deferring some steps to subclasses. I will define an abstract base class `SortAlgorithm` with the `sort` method as the template. This method will contain the control flow (loops) and delegate the specific comparison and swapping logic to abstract methods that concrete subclasses (`BubbleSort`, `SelectionSort`) must implement.

[[ ## response ## ]]
```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class SortAlgorithm(ABC):
    def sort(self, data):
        n = len(data)
        for i in range(n):
            for j in range(0, n - i - 1):
                if self.compare(data[j], data[j + 1]):
                    self.swap(data, j, j + 1)
        return data

    @abstractmethod
    def compare(self, a, b):
        pass

    def swap(self, data, i, j):
        data[i], data[j] = data[j], data[i]

class BubbleSort(SortAlgorithm):
    def compare(self, a, b):
        return a > b

class SelectionSort(SortAlgorithm):
    # Note: Using template structure to mimic logic flow
    def sort(self, data):
        n = len(data)
        for i in range(n):
            min_idx = i
            for j in range(i + 1, n):
                if self.compare(data[min_idx], data[j]):
                    min_idx = j
            self.swap(data, i, min_idx)
        return data

    def compare(self, a, b):
        return a > b

# Testing
if __name__ == "__main__":
    arr1 = [64, 34, 25, 12, 22]
    arr2 = ["banana", "apple", "cherry"]

    bubble = BubbleSort()
    print("Bubble Sort:", bubble.sort(arr1.copy()))

    selection = SelectionSort()
    print("Selection Sort:", selection.sort(arr2.copy()))
```

